art there and that's why it was a  dirty trick to say to Bernie wait wait  and you're okay with the Boston bomber  voting because the reality is not about  the Boston bomber or Timothy McVeigh as  the most important thing if you're in  prison for marijuana possession which in  some places is criminal in some places  is a civil infraction in depending on  the state should you not be able to vote  because you're in prison for marijuana  possession if you stole a television and  later were caught with pot and you're  now a repeat offender and you're in  prison I'm fine with that person voting  I don't believe that that justifies  taking away a right that so many people  worked so hard to obtain and is a key  part of being a participant in society  you very quickly realize when you  actually start going through the  scenarios that most people in prison  should have the right to vote so we  shouldn't start with the edge cases or  the worst cases like should the Boston  bomber get a chance to vote should  timothy mcveigh if he was alive and in  prison get a chance to vote we shouldn't  default to take voting rights away from  everyone in prison because there are  murderers without the possibility of  parole and they really shouldn't be  voting well maybe if you have no  possibility of parole you're not allowed  to vote but most people in prison are  not murderers and I would actually go  even further  I believe that letting imprisoned  Americans vote has positive  externalities getting people involved in  the political system is going to reduce  the percentage of people that go back to  prison for crimes the more involved you  are the more you have something not to  lose hope over the more you have  community the more you feel like you  have a stake in what is going on around  you the less likely you are to commit  crime and that's why I'm fine having the  debate about should everyone in prison  get to vote I understand that some of  these more extreme cases maybe the  an